  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Lead-Acid Knowledge · Practical · Last reviewed 2026-09-16</p>
      <h1>Battery Terminal Types: SAE Post, JIS, Stud and Side</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The battery's power exits through a small piece of lead whose shape varies by market and vehicle — and the
        car's cable clamps were built for exactly one of those shapes.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>At a glance:</strong> The main terminal families: <strong>SAE post</strong> (round tapered posts,
        the universal automotive standard), <strong>JIS</strong> (pencil-thin taper, Japanese vehicles), <strong>stud /
        bolt</strong> (threaded posts, trucks, marine, dual-purpose), and <strong>side terminal</strong> (flush
        GM-style bolts). The terminal type is a <strong>fit requirement</strong> — the cables decide which one
        connects.
      </div>

      <h2>The four families</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Look</th><th>Typical vehicles</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>SAE post</strong></td><td>Round tapered post, top</td><td>Most cars, universal</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>JIS taper</strong></td><td>Thin pencil-size post</td><td>Japanese cars (see <a href="/lead-acid/battery-group-sizes/">JIS groups</a>)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Stud / bolt</strong></td><td>Threaded stud, top or side</td><td>Trucks, marine, heavy duty</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Side terminal</strong></td><td>Flush bolt on the case side</td><td>GM vehicles, tight under-hood layouts</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Why the fit is non-negotiable</h2>
      <p>
        The cable clamps are made for one terminal shape — a JIS clamp does not grip an SAE post properly, and an
        adapter is a workaround, not a repair. The terminal therefore joins the
        <a href="/lead-acid/battery-size-selection/">group size</a> as the two fit checks that come before every
        other spec (see the <a href="https://data.worldbatteryhub.com/dimensions-terminals/">terminal reference</a>
        for the per-group details).
      </p>
      <h2>The post dimensions</h2>
      <p>
        The taper is the identification: SAE posts are tapered cones with the <strong>positive post slightly
        larger</strong> than the negative (top diameters around <strong>~17.5 mm vs ~15.9 mm</strong>), so the
        clamps physically cannot be reversed; JIS pencil posts are thinner (roughly <strong>~12.7 mm vs
        ~11.1 mm</strong>) with the same positive-larger rule. The size difference is the battery's own
        polarity guard (see <a href="https://data.worldbatteryhub.com/dimensions-terminals/">the terminal
        reference</a>).
      </p>


      <h2>Why This Matters</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>I would argue:</strong> The terminal type is the smallest part of the battery and one of the most
          consequential — a wrong terminal cannot be corrected by better specs, which makes it a check that
          belongs before any rating comparison.</p>
        <p>
          <strong>The reasoning:</strong> Fit is binary: the clamp either grips or it does not, and a bad connection at
          the terminal wastes everything the battery delivers. Ten seconds of terminal identification prevents the
          most avoidable purchase error in the category.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is the author's editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

      
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>What are the different battery terminal types?</h3>
      <p>The main families: SAE post (round tapered posts, the universal automotive type), JIS (pencil-size taper, Japanese cars), stud/bolt (threaded posts, trucks and marine), and side terminal (flush GM-style bolts). The car’s cables decide which fits.</p>
      <h3>Why do terminal types matter when buying a battery?</h3>
      <p>Because the car’s cable clamps were built for one terminal shape. A battery with the wrong terminal type will not connect properly — the terminal, like the group size, is a fit requirement, not a preference.</p>
<h2>What buyers should ask</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Which terminal type — SAE post, JIS, stud or side — does the vehicle's cables actually require?</li>
  <li>Is the terminal position and polarity (positive-left or right) correct for the cable reach?</li>
  <li>Does the hold-down and cable lug fit the exact terminal geometry, not just the group size?</li>
  <li>Will an adapter be needed, or does the correct terminal avoid a dangerous loose connection?</li>
</ul>
<h2>The bottom line</h2>
<p>Terminal type is a fit requirement, not a preference — the wrong post or position means a loose or reversed connection. Match the terminal family, position and polarity to the vehicle, because a bad terminal fit is a starting and safety failure waiting to happen.</p>

<h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>Cross-verified from BCI and JIS terminal specifications and manufacturer documentation.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>BCI / JIS terminal specifications.</li>
        <li>Manufacturer fitment documentation — terminal per group.</li>
      </ul>

